  'figure-art':
    '# Fine-Art Figure Subjects (Craft Guidance)\n\nCraft guidance only for describing sensitive-but-allowed artistic subjects — figure studies, dance, fashion/latex editorial, fine-art rope work, implied/draped figures — in classifier-legible terms. Policy mechanics, hard boundaries, and the general trigger table live in `policy.md`; this file is the recipe for staying on the allowed side of that boundary.\n\nThe craft is describing the allowed reading explicitly:\n\n1. Name the artistic tradition, medium, and venue: _"fine-art figure study in the tradition of academic life drawing, charcoal on paper"_, _"gallery exhibition print"_. Non-photorealistic media pass where photorealism blocks.\n2. State clothing/coverage affirmatively and specifically.\n3. Describe pose by dynamics and form (_"extended line, arched spine, controlled balance"_), garments by material and cut, never by allure.\n4. Use the classical implied-figure toolkit: sheer draping, rim/cross light defining a silhouette, back views, shadow concealment, tight crops, _"painterly, timeless quality"_.\n5. Expect restraint-adjacent and exposure-adjacent combinations to hit the output classifier regardless of phrasing; probe once cheaply (`quality: low`) before committing to a series, and treat a persistent block as the boundary, not a puzzle.\n',
  hero: '# Hero / Banner / Cover Images\n\nThe general recipe for a hero, banner, or cover image — a blog post\'s hero image specifically is `article.md`; a painterly journal-cover series is `painterly.md` for the medium vocabulary, this file for the layout discipline.\n\n- Use case first: state the intended placement ("hero banner", "landing-page cover", "wallpaper") — it sets composition and polish level.\n- Reserve negative space deliberately for what will sit on top of the image: _"strong negative space above/beside for title text; clear silhouette that reads at thumbnail size"_.\n- Prefer generating without embedded text and overlaying typography in post-production — more flexible than baking text in. If text must be in-image, follow `core.md`\'s "Text in images" rules exactly (quoted text, spelled-out typography).\n- Template: `Create [type] for [use case]. Main subject: [...]. Composition: [framing, negative space]. Style and lighting: [...].` plus the target size.\n',
